{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjVPh4uEyCsWiEG6QXG2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VaF3QGxhQq3rpiyEBqwSnQ","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Bar 1 of the line shows two D4 notes with tremolo strokes, a whole bar of repeated D4s. It matches the edition's tremolo bars 33–35. The next bar fixes which one it is. Bar 2 of the line has three beamed D4 8ths (one triplet beat), then a D4 8th and an 8th rest. That is bar 36: one triplet group, then D4 8th and an 8th rest. So bar 1 of the line is bar 35. Bars 3 and 4 of the line are whole-bar rests, matching bars 37 and 38. Bar 5 is marked \"for.\" and has a dotted-quarter double stop (E5+A4) and an E4 8th, matching bar 39 [f] E5+A4 quarter., E4 8th. Bar 6 has E4 quarter with an ornament mark below it, then D4 quarter. That is bar 40 (E4 quarter trill, D4 quarter). The barline at the right edge closes the line. Nothing is left over, and nothing goes against the edition.","right":true,"insights":"Bars 33–35 are all the same tremolo, so the first bar can't be placed on its own. Only bar 36's change, ending with a D and a rest, pins it to bar 35. The copyist shortens the tremolo and the triplets, which hides the rhythm. Bar 39's double stop and the trill bar make the line's end easy to place. Bar numbers on the manuscript, or an edition marking the tremolo the way the copyist wrote it, would have made the start certain at a glance."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5549,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5549},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"962679da-92d7-4350-8e9b